  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday night through next Sunday)\nIssued at 1012 PM CDT Sun Oct 11 2026\n\nThe upper low will begin to move across the Great Basin Tuesday \nbefore being centered over the Four Corners by late Tuesday. Upper \nlift will begin to increase over the region as the upper low moves \ncloser to the FA with more widespread convection developing by \nTuesday afternoon. Models, both CAM and synoptic, vary with the \nlocation of convection as it is expected to be more of a line \npositioned mostly parallel to the upper flow. Many models seem to \nfavor the southeastern Rolling Plains, but this will depend upon \nwhere the best upper lift is located. Convection will likely \ncontinue with an increase in coverage Tuesday night into Wednesday \nmorning with convection being fed by a LLJ. This will also \nincrease the possibility for heavy rainfall especially with the \nthreat for training storms. No flood products are expected at this\ntime due to still uncertainties with the overall forecast. \nOrganized severe storms is not forecast, but a few stronger storms\ncould produce severe wind gusts from wet microbursts.\n\nThe overall forecast beyond Wednesday continues to be a mess in \nregard to the future of the upper low. The GFS continues to phase \nthe upper low with the main flow across the Midwest late Wednesday \ninto Thursday with a strong cold front pushing through the FA behind \nthe now open wave trough. The ECMWF attempts to phase the upper low \nbut still isn't quite sold on the idea. It instead absorbs the upper \nlow into a new low centered over Baja all while developing a \nblocking pattern. The ECMWF, however, is now more open to a strong \ncold front unlike previous runs. The GFS has begun to develop a \nblocking pattern as well over the western CONUS, though the upper \nlow portion of the block is weaker than that of the ECMWF.",
